{"time": "2026-10-25T22:12:59", "event": "answer", "session_id": "s-89ec88ad9258", "run_id": "r-752a28facfb8", "report": {"status": "answered", "question_type": "other", "interpretation": "A policy question, not a data query. It asks whether an incident on 10 Feb 2026 is covered when the driver's licence had expired 18 days earlier. Coverage rules follow the incident date, so I used the version of the unlicensed-driving exclusion in force on that date.", "answer": "No. The incident was on 10 Feb 2026, before 1 Mar 2026, so POL-MTR-002 v5.3 applies. Under that version, a licence that had expired for any period is treated as no licence. The exclusion is absolute and has no rebuttal (POL-MTR-002 \u00a73.4, v5.3). An 18-day expiry is therefore not covered, even if the driver renewed the licence soon afterwards. The later v6.0 allows cover to be restored where the licence had expired by 30 days or less and was renewed within 30 days of the incident (POL-MTR-002 \u00a73.4, v6.0). It takes effect on 1 Mar 2026 and does not apply to this incident. I looked only at this exclusion. Other policy terms could also affect the claim.", "answer_sql": "SELECT DATE '2026-02-10' < DATE '2026-03-01' AS v53_applies", "findings": [{"claim": "The incident date of 10 Feb 2026 falls before the v6.0 effective date of 1 Mar 2026, so the v5.3 absolute exclusion governs.", "evidence_sql": "SELECT DATE '2026-02-10' < DATE '2026-03-01' AS v53_applies", "key_numbers": [], "confidence": "high"}], "hypotheses": [], "data_quality_issues": [], "charts": [], "proposed_actions": [], "open_questions": []}, "error": null, "cost_usd": 0.086867, "seconds": 51.08, "models_served": ["claude-sonnet-5-5"], "fell_back": false}
